The second probe lacks the phenolic OH, eliminating efficient phenoxyl-radical formation (B). The residual light-dependent Cy5 signal is therefore produced by a slower, phenol-independent photoreaction common to both probes. Sensitized 417 nm irradiation drives retro-[2+2] cycloreversion of the shared bicyclo[4.2.0] core, directly extruding the electrophilic Michael acceptor methyl (E)-4-oxo-4-(prop-2-yn-1-ylamino)but-2-enoate (C). This fragment alkylates nucleophilic protein side chains at low basal efficiency and carries the alkyne tag that is later read out by CuAAC.

None of the remaining choices satisfy the data: A would give comparable background irrespective of 4-aryl substitution and cannot attach the alkyne; D is not formed under these conditions; E is added identically after irradiation. The structure–activity dependence (residual signal still requires the bicyclo[4.2.0] scaffold) therefore isolates C as the species responsible.

PUBLIC RESPONSE: {C}

